#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063937 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064347 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97dc4044-9984-58a3-bf02-61f31fc31792" :knowledge-point/title "Solving One-Step Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "46597138-36cc-584b-ade8-70cbfafcad2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12905" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4x = y$ for the variable $x$." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate the variable $x$. To do this, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nIn this case, we simply divide both sides of the equation by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\require{cancel} 4x &= y \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac {4x} {4} &= \\dfrac y 4 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac {{\\cancel{4}}x} {\\cancel{4}} &= \\dfrac y 4 \\\\[5pt] x &= \\dfrac y4 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063938 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74cfd55f-4e24-5f97-ba42-28fa410866a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Multi-Step Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "3237ac51-d473-548d-b316-9425717c22bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-12907" :question/problem "Solve the following equation for the variable $x$:\n\n$\\dfrac{3x-2y}{3}=\\dfrac{1+2y}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate the variable $x$. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x - 2y}{3} &= \\frac{1 + 2y}{4} \\\\\n4 \\cdot (3x - 2y) &= 3 \\cdot (1 + 2y) \\\\\n12x - 8y &= 3 + 6y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $8y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x - 8y &= 3 + 6y \\\\\n12x - 8y + 8y &= 3 + 6y + 8y \\\\\n12x &= 3 + 14y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $12$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x &= 3 + 14y \\\\\n\\frac{12x}{12} &= \\frac{3 + 14y}{12} \\\\\n\\frac{12x}{12} &= \\frac{3 + 14y}{12} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3 + 14y}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063950 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064348 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "075c23a4-9de9-59b5-bec4-575b0b0454f4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Two-Step Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "84e24683-73f6-59a1-b2c3-03d3659adf27" :question/math-academy-id "e-13119" :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac{x}{3} + 2y = 1$, express $x$ in terms of $y$." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate the variable $x$. To do this, we treat $y$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $2y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{3} + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x}{3} + 2y - 2y &= 1 - 2y \\\\\n\\frac{x}{3} + 0 &= 1 - 2y \\\\\n\\frac{x}{3} &= 1 - 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{3} &= 1 - 2y \\\\\n3 \\cdot \\frac{x}{3} &= 3 \\cdot (1 - 2y) \\\\\n3 \\cdot \\frac{x}{3} &= 3 \\cdot (1 - 2y) \\\\\nx &= 3 \\cdot (1 - 2y) \\\\\nx &= 3 \\cdot 1 - 3 \\cdot 2y \\\\\nx &= 3 - 6y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
